"Detecting Text Manipulation in Images using Vision Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10278v1)\nciteturn29775view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view0\",\"pattern\":\"Experiments\"}); Total lines: 246\nL188: In this paper, we highlight the motivation and challenges of text manipulation detection. With our experiments, we bridge the gap of lack of text manipulation evaluations in the current literature. As VLMs are getting popular for image manipulation detection, we evaluate several models and can conclude the following: (a) The gap between closed source, GPT-4o and open source VLMs, Qwen-VL, Llama-Vision is large.\nL189: The best performing open source model Qwen-VL trails behind GPT-4o by $7$% on OSTF [cite36†Qu et al.(2025)Qu, Zhong, Guo, and Jin ] and $29$% on FantasyID. To reduce this gap, the responses by GPT-4o can be used to distill the knowledge into open source models and improve them. (b) Specialized VLMs FakeShield [cite46†Xu et al.(2024)Xu, Zhang, Li, Tang, Huang, and Zhang ] and SIDA [cite47†Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Hu, Li, He, Zhao, Peng, Wu, Huang, and Cheng ] fail to generalize to text manipulation.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdversarial robustness through Lipschitz-Guided Stochastic Depth in Neural Networks. (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10298v1)\nciteturn29775view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view1\",\"pattern\":\"Lipschitz\"}); Total lines: 433\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Stochastic Depth L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Lipschitz-Constrained Networks L20:     3. cite10†2.3 Randomization for Robustness L21:   4. cite11†3 Methodology L22:     1. cite12†3.1 Problem Formulation L23:     2. cite13†3.2 Expected Lipschitz Bound L24:     3. cite14†3.3 Depth-Dependent Schedule L25:   5. cite15†4 Experiments L26:     1. cite16†4.1 Baseline Models L27:     2. cite17†4.2 Lipschitz-Based Schedule L28:     3. cite18†4.3 Evaluation Metrics L29:     4. cite19†4.4 Empirical Evaluation of Lipschitz Constants L30:     5. cite20†4.5 Results and Discussion L31:   6. cite21†5 Limitations and Future Work L32:   7. cite22†References L33: cite23†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L34: \nL35: arXiv:2509.10298v1 [cs.CV] 12 Sep 2025\nL36: # Adversarial robustness through Lipschitz-Guided Stochastic Depth in Neural Networks.\nL37: Laith Nayal  Mahmoud Mousatat  Bader Rasheed Affiliation: Laboratory of Multimodal Research in Industry, Innopolis University, 420500 Innopolis, Russia Affiliation: LLC NUHA TECH, 422002, Republic of Tatarstan (Tatarstan), Arsky, Arsk, Chulpan st., 20 Affiliation: Laboratory of Innovative Technologies for Processing Video Content, Innopolis University, 420500 Innopolis, Russia\nL38: l.nayal@innopolis.university  m.mousatat@nuhaai.com  b.rasheed@innopolis.university\nL39: ###### Abstract\nL40: Deep neural networks and Vision Transformers achieve state-of-the-art performance in computer vision but are highly vulnerable to adversarial perturbations. Standard defenses often incur high computational cost or lack formal guarantees. We propose a Lipschitz-guided stochastic depth (DropPath) method, where drop probabilities increase with depth to control the effective Lipschitz constant of the network.\nL41: This approach regularizes deeper layers, improving robustness while preserving clean accuracy and reducing computation. Experiments on CIFAR-10 with ViT-Tiny show that our custom depth-dependent schedule maintains near-baseline clean accuracy, enhances robustness under FGSM, PGD-20, and AutoAttack, and significantly reduces FLOPs compared to baseline and linear DropPath schedules.\nL42: ## 1 Introduction\nL43: \nL44: Deep neural networks are highly vulnerable to adversarial perturbations cite24†Szegedy et al. (2013) ; cite25†Goodfellow et al. (2014) . Standard defenses, including adversarial training cite26†Madry et al. (2017) , randomized smoothing cite27†Cohen et al. (2019) , and spectral normalization cite28†Miyato et al. (2018) , improve robustness but often incur high computational costs and lack formal guarantees.\nL45: An alternative is robustness by design, e.g., constraining the network’s Lipschitz constant cite29†Cisse et al. (2017) ; cite30†Tsuzuku et al. (2018) , which bounds sensitivity to input perturbations. However, these approaches may reduce expressivity or be expensive.\nL46: We propose a Lipschitz-guided stochastic depth (DropPath) method cite31†Huang et al. (2016) ; cite32†Larsson et al. (2016) , where drop probabilities increase with depth to control the effective Lipschitz constant, regularizing deeper layers that dominate sensitivity. This approach enhances robustness without sacrificing clean accuracy and reduces computational cost.\nL47: Experiments on CIFAR-10 with ViT-Tiny show that our custom drop schedule maintains near-baseline clean accuracy, improves robustness under FGSM, PGD-20, and AutoAttack, and significantly reduces FLOPs compared to baseline and linear stochastic depth.\nL48: ## 2 Related Work\nL49: \nL50: ### 2.1 Stochastic Depth\nL51: \nL52: Stochastic depth cite31†Huang et al. (2016) randomly bypasses layers during training, reducing effective depth and improving convergence. DropPath cite32†Larsson et al. (2016) ; cite33†Cubuk et al. (2020) generalizes this idea for neural architecture search. Existing methods often use linear or manually tuned schedules without theoretical guidance.\nL53: ### 2.2 Lipschitz-Constrained Networks\nL54: \nL55: Controlling the Lipschitz constant bounds output sensitivity to input perturbations, aiding robustness and training stability cite29†Cisse et al. (2017) ; cite30†Tsuzuku et al. (2018) ; cite28†Miyato et al. (2018) ; cite34†Bartlett et al. (2017) . Spectral normalization constrains per-layer norms, but these methods do not directly address stochastic regularization like DropPath.\nL56: ### 2.3 Randomization for Robustness\nL57: \nL58: Randomization improves robustness and generalization, e.g., via dropout cite35†Srivastava et al. (2014) , randomized smoothing cite27†Cohen et al. (2019) , or stochastic activation pruning cite36†Fan et al. (2021) . Stochastic depth is a structural randomization method, though its connection to Lipschitz continuity is less explored.\nL59: ## 3 Methodology\nL60: \nL61: Our goal is to design a principled stochastic depth (DropPath) schedule that bounds the effective Lipschitz constant of a deep network. By controlling layer drop probabilities, we aim to improve adversarial robustness while reducing computation.\nL62: ### 3.1 Problem Formulation\nL63: \nL64: Consider a feedforward network of $L$ layers, where each layer $l$ has a Lipschitz constant $\\ell_{l}$ (e.g., $\\ell_{l}=\\sigma_{1}(W_{l})$ for linear layers). Without stochastic depth, the network’s Lipschitz constant is\nL65: \nL66:  | $$\\kappa_{\\text{full}}=\\prod_{l=1}^{L}\\ell_{l}.$$  |\nL67: To control sensitivity, we introduce stochastic depth: each layer $l$ is dropped with probability $p(l)$ (replaced by the identity mapping with Lipschitz 1). Let $\\text{mask}_{l}\\in\\{0,1\\}$ indicate whether layer $l$ is active. The effective Lipschitz constant of a sampled sub-network is\nL68: \nL69:  | $$\\kappa(\\text{mask})=\\prod_{l=1}^{L}\\ell_{l}^{\\text{mask}_{l}}.$$  |\nL70: ### 3.2 Expected Lipschitz Bound\nL71: \nL72: The expected contribution of each layer is\nL73: \nL74:  | $$\\mathbb{E}[\\ell_{l}^{\\text{mask}_{l}}]=(1-p(l))\\ell_{l}+p(l)\\cdot 1=1-p(l)+p(l)\\ell_{l}.$$  |\nL75: \nL76: Assuming an upper bound $\\ell_{l}\\leq\\lambda$, we have\nL77: \nL78:  | $$\\mathbb{E}[\\ell_{l}^{\\text{mask}_{l}}]\\leq 1+p(l)(\\lambda-1),$$  |\nL79: \nL80: and the expected network Lipschitz satisfies\nL81:  | $$\\mathbb{E}[\\kappa(\\text{mask})]=\\prod_{l=1}^{L}\\mathbb{E}[\\ell_{l}^{\\text{mask}_{l}}]\\leq\\prod_{l=1}^{L}\\big(1+p(l)(\\lambda-1)\\big)\\leq\\kappa_{\\text{target}}.$$  |\nL82: \nL83: Taking logarithms and using $\\log(1+x)\\approx x$ for small $x$ gives a sufficient condition:\nL84: \nL85:  | $$\\sum_{l=1}^{L}p(l)(\\lambda-1)\\leq\\log\\kappa_{\\text{target}}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\sum_{l=1}^{L}p(l)\\leq\\frac{\\log\\kappa_{\\text{target}}}{\\lambda-1}.$$  |\nL86: ### 3.3 Depth-Dependent Schedule\nL87: \nL88: To better reflect that deeper layers typically contribute more to Lipschitz growth, we adopt a depth-dependent drop schedule:\nL89: \nL90:  | $$p(l)=1-\\kappa_{\\text{target}}^{\\,l/L},\\quad l=1,\\dots,L,$$  |\nL91: \nL92: assigning higher drop probabilities to deeper layers. This ensures that the layers most responsible for Lipschitz growth are regularized more strongly, improving robustness while preserving clean accuracy.\nL93: \nL94: For example, with $L=12$ layers and $\\kappa_{\\text{target}}=0.7$:\nL95:  | $$p(1)\\approx 0.025,\\quad p(6)\\approx 0.043,\\quad p(12)\\approx 0.099,$$  |\nL96: \nL97: so the first layer is rarely dropped, while the last layer has almost a 10% drop probability.\nL98: ## 4 Experiments\nL99: \nL100: We conduct a series of experiments to evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed Lipschitz-based stochastic depth schedule. All experiments are performed on CIFAR-10 using a ViT-Tiny architecture (patch size 16, 12 transformer blocks) with pretrained weights, trained using the AdamW optimizer for up to 150 epochs, a learning rate of 0.001, and batch size 128 on a NVIDIA V100 16 GB GPU. No data augmentation is applied to isolate the effect of the proposed stochastic depth scheduler.\nL101: ### 4.1 Baseline Models\nL102: \nL103: We first establish baselines to contextualize our results. Two baseline models are trained:\nL104: \nL105:   1. 1.\nL106: \nL107: No DropPath: Standard ViT-Tiny trained without stochastic depth.\nL108: \nL109:   2. 2.\nL110: \nL111: Linear Drop Schedule: ViT-Tiny trained with a conventional linear DropPath schedule, increasing linearly up to a maximum drop rate of $0.1$ across depth.\nL112: These baselines are evaluated in terms of clean accuracy on CIFAR-10, as well as robustness to adversarial attacks. Robustness is measured under FGSM, PGD-20, and Auto attacks.\nL113: ### 4.2 Lipschitz-Based Schedule\nL114: \nL115: Next, we implement the proposed Lipschitz-based schedule. Unlike the uniform formulation, here the drop probability increases with depth:\nL116: \nL117:  | $$p(l)=1-\\kappa_{\\text{target}}^{l/L},\\quad l\\in\\{1,\\dots,L\\}.$$  |\nL118: ### 4.3 Evaluation Metrics\nL119: \nL120: We evaluate models on both standard accuracy, adversarial robustness, and computational efficiency:\nL121: \nL122:   * •\nL123: \nL124: Clean Accuracy: Standard top-1 classification accuracy on the unperturbed test set.\nL125: \nL126:   * •\nL127: \nL128: FGSM: Accuracy under Fast Gradient Sign Method (FGSM) attacks with perturbation magnitudes.\nL129: \nL130:   * •\nL131: \nL132: PGD-20: Accuracy under 20-step Projected Gradient Descent (PGD) attacks with perturbation magnitudes.\nL133: \nL134:   * •\nL135: AutoAttack: Robust accuracy against the four standard components of AutoAttack (APGD-CE, APGD-T, FAB, and Square Attack).\nL136: \nL137:   * •\nL138: \nL139: FLOPs: Floating Point Operations measured per forward pass to evaluate computational cost and efficiency of different DropPath schedules.\nL140: ### 4.4 Empirical Evaluation of Lipschitz Constants\nL141: \nL142: To empirically verify that our custom depth-dependent stochastic depth reduces the effective Lipschitz constant, we measured local Lipschitz constants using the following procedure. For each input batch, we apply a small perturbation $\\delta$ of magnitude $\\epsilon=1/255$ (in $L_{2}$ norm) and compute the ratio between the change in the network output and the input perturbation:\nL143:  | $$L_{\\text{local}}=\\frac{\\|f(x+\\delta)-f(x)\\|_{2}}{\\|\\delta\\|_{2}}.$$  |\nL144: ### 4.5 Results and Discussion\nL145: Table 1: Empirical local Lipschitz statistics for different training strategies (eps = 1/255, $L_{2}$ norm).\nL146: Model  | Mean  | Median  | Max\nL147: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL148: Baseline (No DropPath)  | 0.0111  | 0.0006  | 0.2492\nL149: Linear Stochastic Depth  | 0.0153  | 0.0010  | 0.2766\nL150: Custom Depth-Dependent DropPath  | 0.0119  | 0.0007  | 0.2601\nL151: Table 2: Clean accuracy, adversarial robustness (%), and efficiency of different training strategies on CIFAR-10. Robustness is reported under FGSM, PGD-20, and AutoAttack (APGD-CE and other AA variants) at $\\epsilon=8/255$. Drop path probabilities are included for stochastic depth methods. FLOPs are reported in billions (G).\nL152: Method  | Clean  | FGSM  | PGD-20  | APGD-CE  | Other AA  | FLOPs (G)\nL153: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL154: Baseline  | 91.00  | 46.5  | 30.4  | 29.2  | 28.5  | 1.075\nL155: Linear Stoch. Depth (p=0.1)  | 89.27  | 44.5  | 26.0  | 24.9  | 24.1  | 0.978\nL156: Custom Stoch. Depth (ours, $\\kappa=0.7$)  | 90.88  | 49.2  | 33.1  | 32.1  | 31.1  | 0.273\nL157: The table cite37†2 compares three training strategies on CIFAR-10: baseline ViT, linear stochastic depth, and our custom stochastic depth. While linear stochastic depth improves adversarial robustness over the baseline, it reduces clean accuracy (89.27% vs 91.0%). In contrast, our custom stochastic depth nearly preserves clean accuracy (90.88%) while achieving the highest robustness across FGSM, PGD-20, and AutoAttack variants.\nL158: Additionally, it drastically reduces FLOPs to 0.273G compared to 1.075G for the baseline, demonstrating improved efficiency. Overall, the custom stochastic depth provides a strong balance: maintaining clean performance, enhancing robustness, and significantly lowering computational cost, highlighting the effectiveness of carefully designed drop path strategies.\nL159: The empirical local Lipschitz statistics (Table cite38†1 ) indicate that our custom depth-dependent DropPath schedule maintains stability comparable to the baseline for typical inputs (median $\\sim 10^{-3}$), while slightly increasing mean and maximum values.\nL160: This suggests that, although the custom schedule does not strictly reduce Lipschitz constants for all inputs, it effectively targets deeper layers to enhance robustness and computational efficiency without harming clean accuracy, achieving a favorable robustness–efficiency trade-off.\nL161: ## 5 Limitations and Future Work\nL162: While our custom depth-dependent stochastic depth improves adversarial robustness and maintains clean accuracy, a key question remains: have we truly bounded the global Lipschitz constant of the network? Current evidence comes from empirical local Lipschitz estimates and robustness metrics, which suggest that deeper layers are effectively regularized. However, a formal verification of the overall Lipschitz bound is still an open problem.\nL163: Future work will focus on developing rigorous methods to measure and guarantee global Lipschitz constraints, potentially combining analytical bounds with empirical estimations. Exploring this direction could provide stronger theoretical guarantees and further enhance robustness while preserving efficiency.\nL164: ## References\nL165:   * Szegedy et al. [2013] Christian Szegedy, Wojciech Zaremba, Ilya Sutskever, Joan Bruna, Dumitru Erhan, Ian Goodfellow, and Rob Fergus. Intriguing properties of neural networks. arXiv preprint arXiv:1312.6199, 2013.\nL166:   * Goodfellow et al. [2014] Ian J Goodfellow, Jonathon Shlens, and Christian Szegedy. Explaining and harnessing adversarial examples. arXiv preprint arXiv:1412.6572, 2014.\nL167:   * Madry et al. [2017] Aleksander Madry, Aleksandar Makelov, Ludwig Schmidt, Dimitris Tsipras, and Adrian Vladu. Towards deep learning models resistant to adversarial attacks. arXiv preprint arXiv:1706.06083, 2017.\nL168:   * Cohen et al. [2019] Jeremy Cohen, Elan Rosenfeld, and Zico Kolter. Certified adversarial robustness via randomized smoothing. In international conference on machine learning, pages 1310–1320. PMLR, 2019.\nL171:   * Tsuzuku et al. [2018] Yusuke Tsuzuku, Issei Sato, and Masashi Sugiyama. Lipschitz-margin training: Scalable certification of perturbation invariance for deep neural networks. Advances in neural information processing systems, 31, 2018.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNotebook for the Touché Lab at CLEF 2025 (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.14256v1)\nciteturn29775view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view2\",\"pattern\":\"Evaluation\"}); Total lines: 533\nL198: These prompting strategies ensure controlled generation behavior, consistent quality evaluation, and reliable preference pair construction for training under the ORPO framework.\nL262: By combining RAG’s relevance with CAG’s efficiency and stability, our formulation ensures that the LLM receives a coherent and context-rich prompt that balances factual grounding with consistent advertisement integration. This dual mechanism is particularly effective in stealth advertisement generation where response quality and subtlety must be jointly optimized.\nL263: ### 2.5 Training and Evaluation Strategy\nL264: Our approach to model development was guided by the need for high-context retention, stealthy ad integration, and preference-aligned generation. To meet these requirements, we selected a Mistral-7Bcite58†Jiang et al. (2023) model as the base generator, given its strong instruction-following performance, efficient decoding, and support for large context windows (up to 4,000 tokens in our setup via Unslothcite67†Daniel Han and team (2023) ).\nL463: Hyperparameter  | Value  | Rationale\nL464: Batch Size  | 32  | Efficient GPU usage without overfitting\nL465: Epochs  | 1  | Minimal gains beyond 1 epoch; avoids overfitting\nL466: Learning Rate  | $5\\times 10^{-5}$  | Standard for transformer fine-tuning\nL467: Warmup Steps  | 10  | Stabilizes early updates\nL468: Optimizer  | AdamW  | Suitable for transformer training with weight decay\nL469: Scheduler  | Cosine  | Enables smooth convergence\nL470: Precision  | FP16/BF16  | Reduces memory footprint, speeds up training\nL471: ### 3.3 Model Evaluation\nL472: Model performance was evaluated on a held-out test set from the Webis Native Ads 2024 dataset cite63†Schmidt et al. (2024) . Each response in this set is annotated with a binary label indicating the presence (1) or absence (0) of a native advertisement. To ensure consistency across approaches, the test set was preprocessed using the same configuration employed during training.\nL473: For Approach 1, responses were tokenized using the MPNet tokenizer, while Approach 2 followed a prompt-based format using the DeBERTa tokenizer, with dynamic padding handled via HuggingFace’s DataCollatorWithPadding.\nL474: For inference, the CrossEncoder (Approach 1) outputs a scalar probability between 0 and 1, which is thresholded at 0.5 to generate binary predictions. In contrast, the DeBERTa-based classifier (Approach 2) outputs class-wise logits, and the final prediction is determined by applying argmax over these logits. Despite architectural differences, both models are evaluated using the same criteria.\nL475: Evaluation metrics include Precision, Recall, and F1-Score. Model predictions and ground-truth labels were compared after each epoch using a custom BinaryEvaluator (in the CrossEncoder setup) or via PyTorch and scikit-learn evaluation scripts (in the DeBERTa setup). Evaluation results are reported both per class and in terms of macro and micro averages, to ensure a fair and balanced assessment of model performance across imbalanced classes.\nL476: ### 3.4 Results and Analysis\nL477: \nL478: The evaluation results on the test set for both approaches are presented in Table cite78†1 .\nL479: \nL480: Table 1: Evaluation Results on the Test Set for Both Approaches\nL481: Model  | Precision  | Recall  | F1-Score\nL482: DeBERTa Fine-Tuned  | 0.788  | 0.758  | 0.773\nL483: MPNet CrossEncoder Fine-Tuned  | 0.977  | 0.346  | 0.511\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSME-TEAM: Leveraging Trust and Ethics for Secure and Responsible Use of AI and LLMs in SMEs (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10594v1)\nciteturn29775view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view3\",\"pattern\":\"Methodology\"}); Total lines: 239\nNo matching text found for \"Methodology\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbduct, Act, Predict:Scaffolding Causal Inference for Automated Failure Attribution in Multi-Agent Systems (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10401v1)\nciteturn29775view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view4\",\"pattern\":\"intervention\"}); Total lines: 240\nL37: A2P explicitly guides a large language model through a formal three-step reasoning process within a single inference pass: (1) Abduction, to infer the hidden root causes behind an agent’s actions; (2) Action, to define a minimal corrective intervention; and (3) Prediction, to simulate the subsequent trajectory and verify if the intervention resolves the failure. This structured approach leverages the holistic context of the entire conversation while imposing a rigorous causal logic on the model’s analysis.\nL51: To bridge this gap, we introduce Abduct-Act-Predict (A2P), a novel prompting framework that reframes failure attribution from pattern recognition into a structured causal inference task. Instead of asking for a direct answer, A2P guides an LLM through a formal, three-step counterfactual reasoning process within a single inference pass, operationalizing the logic of Pearl’s structural causal model hierarchy (cite26†Pearl, 2009 ).\nL52: The framework compels the model to: (1) Abduct, inferring hidden factors (e.g., a flawed assumption) that explain a problematic action; (2) Act, defining a minimal, concrete corrective intervention; and (3) Predict, simulating the subsequent counterfactual trajectory to verify if the intervention would have resolved the overall task failure.\nL53: This structured process forces the model to move beyond correlation and rigorously test causal hypotheses, transforming the \"needle-in-the-haystack\" problem (cite27†Liu et al., 2024 ) into a systematic investigation.\nL54: Our approach is not just theoretically sound but empirically dominant. Evaluated on the comprehensive Who&When benchmark (cite22†Zhang et al., 2025 ), A2P Scaffolding achieves a step-level accuracy of 47.46% on the Algorithm-Generated dataset—a 2.85$\\times$ improvement over the 16.67% of its direct baseline. On the more challenging Hand-Crafted dataset, it achieves 29.31% accuracy, a 2.43$\\times$ improvement over the baseline’s 12.07%.\nL87: (2) Action (Defining an Intervention): Once a potential root cause and erroneous action are hypothesized, the framework prompts the LLM to define a minimal, concrete intervention. This corresponds to applying the $do()$-operator in Pearl’s causal calculus (cite48†Pearl et al., 2016 ). The model must specify the exact, “correct” action the agent should have taken in that step. This step is crucial as it translates the abstract hypothesis into a testable, operationalized counterfactual.\nL88: (3) Prediction (Simulating the Counterfactual Trajectory): With the intervention defined, the final step is to predict its consequences. The LLM is instructed to simulate the subsequent 3-5 turns of the conversation under the counterfactual condition that the correct action was taken. It must then predict whether this new, simulated trajectory would lead to the successful completion of the original task.\nL89: This step directly evaluates the decisive nature of the error; if the simulated outcome is success, the hypothesis is confirmed.\nL90: Mathematically, A2P Scaffolding approximates the estimation of a counterfactual outcome $Z(\\mathcal{I}_{(i,t)}(\\tau))$ for an intervention at step $t$. We formalize the failure attribution task within Pearl’s SCM framework where a trajectory $\\tau$ is generated by structural equations with states evolving as $s_{t+1}=f(s_{t},a_{t},\\epsilon_{t})$, where $\\epsilon_{t}$ represents unobserved exogenous variables (e.g., agent’s internal knowledge state).\nL91: The final outcome $Z(\\tau)$ is a function of the full trajectory. Our objective is to find the earliest pair $(i^{*},t^{*})=\\arg\\min_{(i,t)}t$ such that the LLM’s guided simulation predicts $Z(\\mathcal{I}_{(i,t)}(\\tau))=0$ (success). The A2P framework guides the LLM through three approximations:\nL92:  | Abduction:  | $\\displaystyle\\epsilon_{t}\\leftarrow\\arg\\max_{\\epsilon}P(\\epsilon|s_{0:t},a_{t},Z(\\tau)=1)$  |  | (1)\nL93:  | Action:  | $\\displaystyle do(a_{t}\\leftarrow a_{t}^{*})$  |  | (2)\nL94:  | Prediction:  | $\\displaystyle Z(\\tau^{*})=g(s_{0},\\ldots,s_{t},s_{t+1}^{*},\\ldots)\\text{ where }s_{t+1}^{*}=f(s_{t},a_{t}^{*},\\epsilon_{t})$  |  | (3)\nL108: Step-Level Accuracy quantifies the percentage of correctly identified decisive error steps, imposing significantly higher precision requirements on the attribution algorithms. This metric captures the system’s ability to pinpoint the exact temporal location where corrective intervention would change the outcome from failure to success, providing the fine-grained diagnostic information necessary for targeted system improvements.\nL137: The Prediction step demonstrates even greater importance, particularly for complex scenarios. Its removal causes degradation of 7.14 percentage points on Algorithm-Generated and a substantial 12.07 percentage points on Hand-Crafted step accuracy. This validates our theoretical framework that explicit counterfactual simulation—testing whether a corrective intervention would resolve the failure—is essential for distinguishing decisive errors from incidental mistakes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnderstanding Outer Optimizers in Local SGD: Learning Rates, Momentum, and Acceleration (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10439v1)\nciteturn29775view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view5\",\"pattern\":\"Assumption\"}); Total lines: 1234\nL125: ### 3 Theory\nL126: \nL127: In this section we conduct the study our main algorithm, Generalized Local SGD (Algorithm cite40†1 with $\\mathrm{LocalUpdate}(y,g)=y-\\eta g$ and $\\mathrm{OuterUpdate}(x,\\Delta)=x-\\gamma\\Delta$). We first review some preliminaries, then present our main results.\nL128: \nL129: #### 3.1 Preliminaries\nL130: \nL131: We are solving the optimization problem $\\displaystyle\\min_{x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(x)$, where we assume $f$ satisfies the following curvature and regularity condition.\nL132: ###### Assumption 3.1.\nL133: \nL134: The function $f$ is differentiable, convex, has $L$-Lipschitz gradients, and has a minimizer $x_{\\ast}$.\nL135: \nL136: We suppose that we can access a stochastic first-order oracle that given a point $x$ returns a gradient $g(x)$ that satisfies the following assumption.\nL137: ###### Assumption 3.2.\nL138: \nL139: Given a point $x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, the stochastic gradients $g(x)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ are (a) unbiased in expectation $\\mathbb{E}\\left[g(x)\\right]=\\nabla f(x)$, and (b) has variance bounded as $\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\left\\lVert g(x)-\\nabla f(x)\\right\\rVert^{2}\\right]\\leq\\sigma^{2}$, where $\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\cdot\\right]$ denotes the expectation operator.\nL140: Our setting is distributed, but with identically distributed data: there are $M$ different nodes, but they all sample stochastic gradients from the same data distribution in an i.i.d. (independent and identically distributed) manner. We denote the inner product between two vectors $a$ and $b$ by $\\left\\langle a,b\\right\\rangle$ and by $\\left\\lVert\\cdot\\right\\rVert$ the corresponding Euclidean norm. For the purpose of theoretical analysis, can write Generalized Local SGD succinctly as\nL141:  | $\\displaystyle\\vskip-2.84526pty_{m,r,0}$  | $\\displaystyle=x_{r},\\qquad g_{m,r,h}=\\text{Stochastic gradient of $y_{m,r,h}$}$  |\nL142:  | $\\displaystyle y_{m,r,h+1}$  | $\\displaystyle=y_{m,r,h}-\\eta g_{m,r,h},\\text{ for }m=1,\\ldots,M\\text{ in parallel and }h=0,1,\\ldots,H-1\\text{ in sequence. }$  |\nL143:  | $\\displaystyle x_{r+1}$  | $\\displaystyle=x_{r}-\\gamma\\eta\\sum_{h=0}^{H-1}\\frac{1}{M}\\sum_{m=1}^{M}g_{m,r,h}.$  |  | (GEN-LOC-SGD)\nL267: Theorem cite56†3.8 allows us to observe another potential benefit of using $\\gamma\\neq 1$. To see how, let us make the simplifying assumption that $\\left\\lVert\\hat{g}_{r,h}\\right\\rVert\\approxeq G_{1}$ and $\\left\\lVert g_{m,r,h}\\right\\rVert\\approxeq G_{2}$.\nL1000: Let $\\mathbb{E}_{r,h}\\left[\\cdot\\right]$ denote the expectation conditional on all information up to and including round $r$ and local step $h$. Then,\nL1001: \nL1002:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}_{r,h}\\left[X_{r,h}\\right]$  | $\\displaystyle=0.$  |\nL1003: \nL1004: Furthermore, we have by the triangle inequality, then our assumption on the noise followed by Lemma cite173†B.1 that almost surely\n"
